WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
YUFENG GUO: Natural language has
many challenges that are unique

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.900
and separate it from other
data types like images

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.250
and structured data.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.880
So it requires a slightly
different approach.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.670
Today, we'll explore
a foundational piece

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.330
of modeling natural language,
called "bag of words."

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.210
What does it mean?

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.540
And how do we use
it to process text?

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.380
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.754
[THEME SONG]

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.590
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:38.010
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.380
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.690
And on this episode,
we're going to look

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:47.200
at how to use bag of words
to classify natural language.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.280
Natural language is
special because it

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.910
has structure inherent in the
language while at the same time

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.320
being very free-form.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.230
There are many ways you
can say the same thing.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.440
And you can also say
very similar words,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.990
and yet mean very
different things.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:04.760
So in much of
machine learning, we

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.140
aim to turn our data
into matrices or tensors.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.660
This is very natural for
images since that's already

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.360
their inherent representation.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.090
Structured data often
meets a similar fate,

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.780
with numbers in a spreadsheet
mapping very directly to input

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.310
matrix values.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.950
But with natural
language, we need

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.570
to somehow find a way to turn
words into numbers so we can

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.250
stick them into those matrices.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.190
There are many ways
that we can do this.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:39.810
And today, we'll focus on an
approach called "bag of words."

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.450
Let's pretend for a moment
that we're learning English

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.500
for the first time ever.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.700
And for some reason,
the first words

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.180
we have chosen to learn
in our entire vocabulary

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.580
are these 10 shown here--

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:56.700
words like "dataframe" and
"graph," "plot," "color,"

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.380
and "activation."

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.230
And so we want to
be able to identify,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.040
given some arbitrary
text, whether that topic

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.070
is about pandas,
keras, or Matplotlib.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.680
How might we do that?

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.920
Perhaps if we looked at
a sentence, like "how

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.130
to plot dataframe
bar graph," we would

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:23.405
recognize just the words "plot,"
"dataframe," and "graph."

00:02:23.405 --> 00:02:26.750
The rest of the sentence would
look like a foreign language,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.250
just gibberish.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.440
Knowing only those three
words in this sentence,

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.660
though, we might
still be able to get

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:36.210
some sense of what it's about.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.690
And the way you might capture
this information in an array

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.530
or matrix would be to first
make an array that represents

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.180
your entire vocabulary.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.360
So in this case, we have
an array of just length 10.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.720
We'd set all those
values to 0 and turn

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.910
on the array indices
that correspond

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:57.410
to the words in the sentence
by setting them to 1.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.220
Notice that this has
nothing to do with the order

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.286
the words appear in
the input sentence,

00:03:03.286 --> 00:03:05.830
but everything to do with
the order of the words

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.200
in our vocabulary list.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.850
So now we've encoded or
translated the English sentence

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:17.380
into an array of numbers
based on our somewhat limited

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.010
understanding of English.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.920
The words we don't
recognize, we'll just ignore.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.410
Notice that this has the
effect of scrambling up

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:30.400
the order of the words,
like, say, a bag of words.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.490
Of course, we should do
the same for our labels.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.750
This is much simpler, since
there are only three of them.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.910
In our case, we
have some sentences

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.770
that have more than
one label attached

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.720
to them at the
same time, however,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.800
since a sentence can talk
about multiple topics at once.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.700
In that case, we want to set
all the relevant indices to 1,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.550
leaving the rest as 0, just
like we did for the words

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.020
from our training data.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.240
Now we've turned the
inputs as well as

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.090
the outputs, which
both used to be words,

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.310
into arrays of numbers.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:07.060
And we can let machine
learning do what it does best--

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.390
map one set of numbers to
another set of numbers.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.870
All the heavy lifting is
done in the preprocessing

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.640
as we transformed
or encoded that text

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.272
into numerical representations.

00:04:18.272 --> 00:04:21.899
Bag of words is a pretty simple
approach for doing this task,

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:25.020
though it's worth pointing
out that it might surprise you

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.030
how well it works
in some situations.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:32.760
How might we build a bag
of words modeled with code?

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.810
Keras has a convenient
preprocessing library

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:39.320
that we can use to handle
much of this for us.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.720
Using the Tokenizer
class, we can

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.760
select the size
of the vocabulary

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.140
we'd like to utilize.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.740
In our example, we just had 10
words, which is quite small,

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.190
but in our code, let's choose
something bigger, like 400.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.030
This will then be fit on
the entire body of the text

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.260
from your training
data, selecting out

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.520
the most common 400 words.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.910
With the tokenization process
complete, building the model

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.010
becomes quite
straightforward and similar

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.230
to working with other
structured data.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:11.890
Since each row is now just
an input of 1s and 0s,

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:15.340
using something as simple as
a standard, fully connected,

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.370
deep neural network
can be quite effective.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.270
If you're planning on having
multiple label classification,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.300
where more than one label might
be true for a single input,

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.680
as we do here, be sure that
we choose a sigmoid activation

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.250
instead of the more common
Softmax activation function,

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:36.990
and pair it with binary
cross-entropy loss.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:37.930
So there you have it--

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.360
the bag of words
model in a nutshell.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.970
Understanding how
bag of words works

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.190
and its advantages
and drawbacks can

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:48.040
help you build your foundation
in natural language processing

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.180
as you move on to more advanced
approaches to encoding text.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.130
For more details
and examples, be

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:57.010
sure to check out the expanded
blog post I have linked below

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:58.780
in the description.

